From 07877d8e8cb005b34bce2c6f08c3a36631fa0fe6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Evans <jacobicusjax@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 19:29:15 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Fix Math Operations Quest navigation, mode-aware copy, and
 hint lifecycle

Focused debug/polish pass on the live Math Operations Quest. No changes to
the math engine, difficulty rules, reward contract, or Dragonswood systems.

- Back link pointed at index-v2.html#games; the live student portal is
  index.html#games. index-v2.html is a reference build, not where students go.
- Easy mode showed "Potential Dragonswood level XP for this round" under a
  card already reading PRACTICE. The support copy is now difficulty-aware, and
  Hard states plainly that it doubles Quest Points, not Player XP.
- renderDivisionBoard() called every exact quotient a "random division problem"
  even in Custom / Worksheet Mode. The board message now follows the mode, and
  the remainder wording reads sensibly in both.
- The hint control stayed on screen after a problem was solved, offering help
  for a step that no longer existed. It is now hidden and disabled whenever no
  step is waiting, and returns when the next problem starts. Post-completion
  clicks were already inert; they can no longer be reached at all.
- The hero subtitle is the permanent product identity again. setOperationUI()
  was overwriting it with per-operation sequences, which made the four-operation
  app look like the old Division-only app. The operation-specific algorithm now
  drives the Core Habit cycle instead, matching the step cards.
- The header overflowed 47px at 360px CSS width, forcing horizontal scroll and
  clipping the left edge of every micro-label. It stacks below 420px now.
- Bumped Math Operations assets to v56.26.0 so caching cannot serve the old
  navigation target, including the portal card link that loads this page.

Verified: scripts/verify-debug.py 14/14; the math-engine regression cases
(587+468, 99999+1, 742-389, 1000-1, 7824-2356, 347x6, 347x26, 999x99, 864/8,
42875/125, 1000/64 R40) and chained-borrow regroup data all pass against the
edited source; Easy/Normal/Hard, Random/Custom and all four operations
exercised in-browser; no horizontal scroll at 360, 390, 768, 1366, or 200% zoom.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
